Open **`template.py`** (in this `LAB` folder). It has the structure already — you fill in the marked sections.

## Choose your lane

Pick **one**. All three are the same program with different words.

| Lane | Your three fields | Example |
|---|---|---|
| **AI / Data Science** | dataset name, rows loaded, rows expected | `survey_2026`, `1187`, `1200` |
| **Cyber Security** | source IP, failed logins, total attempts | `10.0.0.5`, `12`, `400` |
| **IT** | hostname, GB used, GB total | `srv-01`, `87`, `120` |

## What to build

**Threshold — pass standard.** Define `Record` with `__init__` storing all three
fields as attributes — no methods yet. Create at least 2 instances and print a
bordered report for each, directly from their attributes.

**Typical.** Add a `status(self)` method (100%+ `OVER LIMIT`, 90%+ `WARNING`,
otherwise `OK`). Print it as part of each report. Give the method a one-line
docstring.

**Excellent.** Instead of writing records in the code, write `load_records(path)`
that reads `label,value,limit` lines from your own data file and returns a list of
`Record` objects. Process the whole list, and keep a running count of how many
were `OVER LIMIT`, printed once at the end.

## Rules

- **Every method does one job**, with a one-line docstring.
- Attributes are set through `self` in `__init__` — nothing stored as a plain
  local variable that disappears when `__init__` ends.
- Call `status()` once per record, store the result in a variable, then use that variable.

## When it works

1. Run it and check every record's numbers by hand.
2. Check `r1` and `r2` really are independent — change one, print both.
